    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 230955\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n255 AM MST Sun Aug 23 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Isolated to scattered thunderstorms, some strong to possibly\n  even severe, are expected this evening across south-central\n  Arizona. \n\n- Increasingly less favorable conditions for thunderstorms will\n  develop starting Monday with virtually no chances for rain from\n  Tuesday through the rest of the week.\n\n- High temperatures will reach or exceed 110 degrees nearly every\n  day this week and will be near daily records, while morning \n  lows remain unusually warm. \n\n- Extreme Heat Warnings are in effect through Saturday with daily\n  Major to Extreme HeatRisk.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nToday could shape up to be another somewhat busy monsoon day, but\nunlike last Wednesday moisture and the amount of instability will\nbe more limited. Atmospheric conditions at the moment are not \nvery conducive for convection due to the limited moisture, but the\nupper levels are somewhat favorable for development. The\nsubtropical high has shifted far enough to the east (eastern New\nMexico/western Texas) to decrease the subsidence inversion. An\nupper level trough has also moved in along the West Coast placing\nmuch of our region within or near the more favorable right \nentrance region of a weak upper jet. So, despite the overall \nmeager moisture currently in place, other atmospheric ingredients \nare more favorable and better moisture is expected to advect \nnorthward today potentially putting southern and central Arizona \nback in the game for monsoon storms.\n\nGiven the anticipated scenario for late this afternoon and\nevening, we are becoming more confident in our chances for \nisolated to scattered thunderstorms. Part of this confidence comes\nfrom the 06Z HRRR which shows storms developing north of Phoenix \nand over Pima County late this afternoon with both clusters \nsending outflows toward the Phoenix metro. With forecast MUCAPEs \nnear 1000 J/kg and CIN falling to below 100 J/kg late this \nafternoon, any decent outflow collision seems likely to spark off \nadditional storm development. The 06Z HRRR shows this exact \nprogression with storms developing over or near the Phoenix metro \nearly this evening. Additionally, since moisture will be more \nlimited, DCAPEs will be very high (1700-2000 J/kg) supporting \nstrong to locally severe wind gusts. Forecast PoPs have been \nraised into a 20-30% range, which better matches the latest high-\nres guidance. \n\nConvective chances for Monday are even more uncertain as guidance\ngenerally shows at most a 10% chance despite moisture and \ninstability being better than today. However, subsidence will be \nmuch stronger on Monday as the subtropical ridge begins to move \nwestward back into our region. We will have to wait until tonight\nto have a better idea on the storm potential for Monday, but it \ndoes seem to be considerably lower than this afternoon/evening.\n\nTemperatures today will again be right near record highs with\nlower desert readings from 110-115 degrees. The increased boundary\nlayer moisture expected for Monday should hold daytime highs down\na bit with NBM forecast highs currently between 106-111 degrees,\nbut heat indices will mostly match what are expected today.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nForecast attention turns away from monsoon storm potential and\nback toward the extreme heat conditions starting Tuesday. The\nsubtropical high center will continue to shift westward into our\nregion into the middle part of the week with the center likely\nsettling somewhere over southern Arizona from Wednesday through at\nleast Friday or Saturday. Forecast H5 heights are also shown to\nreach 594-597dm for the latter half of the week which places it\njust shy of climatological records for late August. NBM\ntemperature guidance remains quite consistent showing highs rising\ninto a 112-116 degree range for the latter half of this week as\nconditions dry out. Daily temperature records in Phoenix will \ncontinue to be in jeopardy through the entire week, but are likely\nto fall short in Yuma and El Centro. Once the high center moves\noverhead by Wednesday, rain chances will essentially go to 5% or\nless. As of now, conditions for any monsoon storm activity do not\nlook good through next weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0910Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n \nThe main aviation concern will be the gusty outflows and potential\nfor reduced visibilities and thunderstorms. Until then easterly\nwinds will continue through the overnight and morning hours\nshifting out of the west near 19-20Z this afternoon. Another round\nof activity is expected to develop to the northeastern high\nterrain and in southern parts of AZ this afternoon and evening.\nOutflow boundaries will likely develop and push into the metro\nfrom the NE and S. KPHX and KIWA will likely be affected by the\nsouthern outflow between 04-07Z, while models show KSDL and KDVT\naffected by the northeastern outflow a little earlier, near 01-05Z\ntonight. This is reflected in the most recent TAF package but is\nsubject to change in future packages depending on how models\nhandle each outflow accordingly. Chances for direct TS impacts\nthis evening remain at up to 30% chance but enough uncertainty\nremains that no PROB30 was introduced at this point in time. FEW-\nSCT mid/high cloud decks will be common throughout the period.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo major aviation concerns are expected through the TAF period.\nKIPL will maintain west winds through the overnight period then\ngoing SE'rly by 15Z. TS activity over the Baja spine may send an\nsouthwesterly outflow into KIPL by the late afternoon hours, with\na less than 20% chance for direct TS impacts. KBLH will swing\nbetween south to SW'rly winds, favoring the SW. Otherwise FEW-SCT\nmid to high cloud decks will be common through the period. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nHot temperatures will prevail through the entire week with shower\nthunderstorm chances mainly today and possibly Monday. Strong \ngusty winds will be possible late this afternoon and evening with \nany thunderstorms, while chances for wetting rains will remain \nbelow 20%. Minimum RHs will improve through Monday with readings \nof 15-25% today and 20-30% Monday, before dropping back into the\nteens into the middle part of the week. Temperatures will be \naround 5-10 degrees above normal, with lower desert highs \neclipsing 110 degrees nearly every afternoon. Outside of \nthunderstorm outflow winds, winds will follow the typical diurnal \ntendencies with some modest afternoon upslope gustiness.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...\nRecord highs:\n\nDate       Phoenix         Yuma         El Centro\n----       -------         ----         ---------\nAug 23   114 in 2011    115 in 2011    116 in 1969\nAug 24   115 in 2020    119 in 1985    117 in 1985\nAug 25   113 in 2011    116 in 1962    117 in 1962\nAug 26   117 in 2011    115 in 2020    115 in 1981\nAug 27   114 in 2023    120 in 1981    117 in 1981\nAug 28   117 in 2023    118 in 2023    117 in 2009\nAug 29   116 in 2023    117 in 1954    117 in 2005\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Saturday for AZZ530>555-559-\n     560.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Saturday for CAZ562-565>567-\n     569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman \nAVIATION...Lojero/Ryan\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\nCLIMATE...18/Kuhlman\n",
